WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.300
If you are coming to react native from web development,

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:08.600
your natural instinct when building a list is to reach for the standard .map array method.

3
00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.700
On a desktop browser, that works without a second thought.

4
00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.900
But mobile devices operate on strict, unforgiving memory budgets

5
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.600
and they simply don't have the brute processing power of a laptop.

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:24.400
This split-screen comparison shows why standard web mapping fails on mobile.

7
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.100
On the left, a web browser easily renders every item simultaneously.

8
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.300
On the right, the app freezes and drops frames

9
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:37.100
because forcing a massive list into memory all at once overloads the device.

10
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:43.100
To prevent those crashes, we have to map out exactly which react native list API you need to use

11
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:47.400
based entirely on the size of your data and your specific UI layout.

12
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:50.200
Choosing the correct API is a balancing act.

13
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.700
You want to avoid over-engineering a simple static screen

14
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:58.500
while ensuring your complex data-heavy screens actually stay alive under stress.

15
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:04.500
Mastering mobile lists is about respecting the physical limitations of the hardware in your user's pocket.

16
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.000
This flow chart represents the decision tree for mobile lists.

17
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.400
Choices branch based on the user experience you're delivering.

18
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.900
The first branch handles short lists, under 20 items with static data.

19
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.600
Here, you use scroll view, combined with the .map function.

20
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.000
This route gives you the fastest development time

21
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.400
because you don't have to learn any new APIs.

22
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:26.900
You just right react the way you always have.

23
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.000
But it works because of a very specific mechanism.

24
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.900
Scroll view immediately loads all of its data directly into the device's RAM, the second it renders.

25
00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.600
That immediate loading is a strict trade-off.

26
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:44.500
It means this approach is only safe for very short predictable components, like in app settings menu.

27
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:49.300
If you push a scroll view past that 20 item threshold, you guarantee memory bloat.

28
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.600
It has zero dynamic memory management capabilities,

29
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.500
so every item you add constantly eats away at the phone's resources.

30
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:02.100
When your data scales up, we follow the second branch, handling dynamic or long data sets.

31
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.500
This leads us directly to flat list.

32
00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:09.200
Flat list is the primary heavy duty component for rendering data and react native.

33
00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.200
It handles scale through a mechanical advantage called virtualization.

34
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.500
Virtualization solves memory limits.

35
00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.700
Only blocks visible inside the physical screen are colored.

36
00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.700
Everything outside is grayed out.

37
00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:24.200
As the user scrolls, the engine unloads hidden items at the top

38
00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:27.600
and recycles that memory for new items entering the bottom.

39
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.900
The trade-off is that you have to abandon your standard web mapping.

40
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:34.400
Flat list requires you to learn its specific API boilerplate,

41
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.400
mapping your data through properties like render item and key extractor.

42
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.300
Writing that extra boilerplate is the mandatory tax you pay

43
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.500
to achieve fluid professional scrolling on mobile hardware.

44
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:49.800
We hit the third branch when your design requires categorized or grouped data.

45
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:53.300
Think of the alphabetized layout in an iOS context book.

46
00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.600
For this, we use section list.

47
00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:59.000
Section list is built to provide native feeling sticky headers

48
00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.200
and distinctly categorized sections right out of the box.

49
00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:04.400
But it comes with a strict constraint.

50
00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.600
It demands a highly specific nested array data structure to function.

51
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.800
If your backend data arrives flat and isn't pre-formatted for section list,

52
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.500
your app will burn excessive processing power trying to reshape those arrays on the client side

53
00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.400
before it can even render the screen.

54
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.500
The fourth and final branch covers transforming a standard vertical list

55
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:25.600
into a multi-column grit layout.

56
00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:29.000
By applying the num columns property to a standard flat list,

57
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.400
you instantly convert your vertical rows into a rigid grid.

58
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.900
The primary constraint here is flexibility.

59
00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.800
You cannot change the column count dynamically on the fly

60
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.700
if the device rotates or the layout state changes.

61
00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.700
Altering that column count requires you to force a complete re-render

62
00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.700
of the entire list component.

63
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:49.900
Implementing specialized layouts like grids and sections

64
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.000
requires architectural foresight.

65
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.200
You have to strictly format your data state

66
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.700
long before it reaches the UI layer.

67
00:03:56.700 --> 00:04:00.000
If you're a web transplant, relying on standard.map methods

68
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.300
for every list will result in visible stuttering

69
00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.500
once your content exceeds the viewport.

70
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:06.700
For beginners, the best move you can make today

71
00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.700
is accepting the initial learning curve of flat list.

72
00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.200
Doing it now prevents a massive refactoring headache

73
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.900
a month down the line when your user data grows.

74
00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.300
If you are focused on performance optimization,

75
00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:20.400
remember that real speed comes from auditing your UI designs

76
00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.700
before you write a single line of code.

77
00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.600
Proactively deciding that a screen needs a section list

78
00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:28.000
or a num columns grid dictates exactly

79
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.300
how you must structure your backend data queries.

80
00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:33.000
This matrix maps out your action plan.

81
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Under 20 static items means scroll view.

82
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.100
Dynamic data gets flat list.

83
00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:40.500
Grouped data requires section list.

84
00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.500
Grids need num columns.

85
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.100
To practice, check the official React Native docs

86
00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:47.700
for flat list and section list and tax.

87
00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.500
If you prefer a hands-on step-by-step walkthrough,

88
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.500
we highly recommend the comprehensive

89
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.500
eight-hour masterclass by notjust.dev.

90
00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:58.100
You can find the links to both of those technical resources

91
00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:00.000
in the video description below.

92
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.400
Matching the right list component to your data

93
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:05.200
ensures the application handles memory efficiently,

94
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.300
whether you're rendering a short settings menu

95
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.000
or a scrolling feed of thousands.

